The 1.21.1 mappings doesn't have preRemoveSideEffects (that's newer versions). Need the official 1.21.1 behavior for block removal: Level.setBlock / removeBlock calls block.onRemove. Containers.dropContentsOnDestroy is called from Block.onRemove? Let me look in the sources jar. Is there a sources jar for Minecraft? build/moddev/artifacts/neoforge-21.1.262-sources.jar maybe includes Minecraft sources. Let me check. Could unzip, but we can't run shell commands? We have no shell tool anyway. Only read/grep/glob. grep can search inside jar? No.

We need to be careful: judge based on mod source only, and mark vanilla semantics as unverified where appropriate. But maybe work/mcsrc has decompiled sources from earlier. Search for class Block in work dir? Only few files. .mc-src has 3. So no vanilla Block.java.

Key candidate bugs from source reasoning:

1. **Eject button never wired to client UI**: AlchemyFurnaceMenu.clickMenuButton(id=1) exists but AlchemyFurnaceScreen has no button/widget and no call sending button id. So the "退料按钮" entry doesn't exist in the actual client screen. That's a confirmed source fact: no client caller of clickMenuButton in repo. So player cannot trigger eject. Not an item loss per se, but entry unusable. Note must consider maybe keybind elsewhere. grep for `menu.clickMenuButton` etc found nothing. So confirmed.

2. **Closing menu**: items stay in block entity; no drop on close. Removed() only stopOpen. Fine. But progress continues via serverTick. No loss.

3. **Breaking furnace**: onRemove -> dropContents -> Containers.dropContents(level,pos,this) drops all 4 slots including output. Then super.onRemove. In 1.21.1, BaseContainerBlockEntity doesn't drop automatically; Block.onRemove default no-op (playerWillDestroy handles drops for some blocks but not container). So likely single drop. But potential duplication: dropContents drops items from container but doesn't clear them; then block entity removed, so no dup. However `Containers.dropContents` iterates and calls container.removeItem? Actually dropContents(level,pos,container) calls container.clearContent? Let me recall 1.21.1 Containers: 
```java
public static void dropContents(Level level, BlockPos pos, Container container) {
    dropContents(level, (double)pos.getX(), (double)pos.getY(), (double)pos.getZ(), container);
}
public static void dropContents(Level level, double x, double y, double z, Container container) {
    for (int i = 0; i < container.getContainerSize(); i++) {
        dropItemStack(level, x, y, z, container.getItem(i));
    }
}
```
It does NOT clear the container. So items remain in BE. If onRemove is called then BE removed from level, okay. But note: onRemove can be called on chunk unload? No, onRemove called on block change. Also onRemove called when block replaced by same? guarded by state.is(newState.getBlock()). Also called with `movedByPiston`. Fine.

But a possible duplication: `dropContents` also being called while block entity's items remain, and if `level.setBlock` fails? Unlikely.

Another consideration: the output slot stack could contain produced stack — dropped too. Fine.

But wait — there's a real bug candidate in ejectInputs: it drops the output slot too but the eject button is unreachable client-side. Also `player.getInventory().add(copy)` returns boolean in 1.21.1; if partially added? Inventory.add returns true if fully added, and if false it may have partially added some items, then player.drop(copy,false) drops the whole copy again → duplication. Need check vanilla Inventory.add semantics in 1.21.1: 
```java
public boolean add(ItemStack stack) {
    return this.add(-1, stack);
}
public boolean add(int slot, ItemStack stack) {
    if (stack.isEmpty()) return false;
    ...
    int i = this.getSlotWithRemainingSpace(stack); ...
    // if it can't fully merge, it may partially insert then return false? 
}
```
In 1.21.1 Inventory.add: 
```java
public boolean add(int slot, ItemStack stack) {
    if (stack.isEmpty()) {
        return false;
    } else {
        try {
            if (stack.isDamaged()) {
                if (slot == -1) {
                    slot = this.getFreeSlot();
                }
                if (slot >= 0) {
                    this.items.set(slot, stack.copyAndClear());
                    this.items.get(slot).setPopTime(5);
                    return true;
                } else if (this.player.hasInfiniteMaterials()) {
                    stack.setCount(0);
                    return true;
                } else {
                    return false;
                }
            } else {
                int i;
                do {
                    i = stack.getCount();
                    if (slot == -1) {
                        stack.setCount(this.addResource(stack));
                    } else {
                        stack.setCount(this.addResource(slot, stack));
                    }
                } while (!stack.isEmpty() && stack.getCount() < i);
                if (stack.getCount() == i && this.player.hasInfiniteMaterials()) {
                    stack.setCount(0);
                    return true;
                } else {
                    return stack.getCount() < i;
                }
            }
        } ...
```
